<DocsLayout
  title="Core concepts"
  description="Learn the dual-repository model, double revision, tri-state validity, source.paths evidence, and why llmdoc knowledge is reference data."
  section="concepts"
>
  <h1>Core concepts</h1>
  <blockquote>The useful question is not “How much context can the model hold?” but “What context is sufficient for this task?”</blockquote>

  <h2>Context Floor</h2>
  <p>The Context Floor is the richness of context an agent needs to solve the current problem. llmdoc raises information density before broad source exploration, then native tools verify live details such as implementation text, line numbers, tests, and Git state.</p>

  <h2>Two repositories, one direction</h2>
  <p>llmdoc separates facts from understanding. The <strong>Source Git</strong> repository is the authority for what the code does, and it is read-only to llmdoc. The <strong>Knowledge Git</strong> repository is an independent repository that stores durable engineering understanding, and it is the only persistent write boundary.</p>
  <pre><code>Source Git (read-only)  →  Human / Agent  →  Knowledge Git (docs + meta)</code></pre>
  <p>The CLI reads source through fixed Git objects, disables optional index writes, and never falls back to writing knowledge into the source repository. The binding is recorded in a user-level registry, so the source tree stays untouched. The knowledge repository is external by default; nested independent Git is supported only when explicitly selected.</p>

  <h2>What belongs in knowledge</h2>
  <p>Admission requires all three: the knowledge affects future choices, is expensive to reconstruct, and stays valid across commits. Decisions and rationale, boundaries, invariants, cross-module contracts, and non-obvious failure semantics qualify. Source inventories, per-line observations, temporary plans, and raw sessions do not.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Decisions over observations:</strong> documents preserve rationale and constraints; source, tests, schemas, and manifests retain live facts.</li>
    <li><strong>Review coupling:</strong> <code>source.paths</code> maps a change to the documents whose claims may need review, rather than manufacturing a file-coverage score.</li>
    <li><strong>Progressive disclosure:</strong> the map, metadata, selected bodies, and source form successively deeper layers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Progressive reading</h2>
  <p>The retrieval surface reveals knowledge in layers: map or metadata first, then search or source ownership, then selected bodies. A caller may stop at any layer.</p>
  <pre><code>tree / index  →  search / context  →  show</code></pre>

  <h2>Document model</h2>
  <p>Documents are standard Markdown <code>.md</code> files with YAML front matter. The docs-relative POSIX path is the document ID, and <code>docs/</code> may be nested to any depth. The <code>README.md</code> navigation region is generated and is never a knowledge node.</p>
  <pre><code>knowledge/
├── llmdoc.yaml
├── README.md
├── docs/
│   ├── architecture.md
│   └── lifecycle/task-recovery.md
├── inbox/
└── .llmdoc/meta.json</code></pre>
  <p><code>kind</code> is one of <code>architecture</code>, <code>decision</code>, <code>guide</code>, or <code>reference</code>. A decision records Context, Decision, Why, and Consequences, and may point to the decision it supersedes through <code>relations.supersedes</code>.</p>

  <h2>source.paths is the evidence contract</h2>
  <p>Every formal project document must declare a non-empty <code>source.paths</code> list of repo-relative globs. Absolute paths and <code>..</code> are rejected, and concrete paths must exist in the fixed snapshot. A zero-match glob is a diagnostic, never a silent pass.</p>
  <pre><code>kind: decision
description: Why task recovery joins lease and timeout to decide owner expiry.
source:
  paths:
    - internal/task/**
    - pkg/lease/**
relations:
  requires:
    - lifecycle/architecture.md</code></pre>
  <p><code>requires</code> forms an acyclic dependency graph. When an upstream document changes and is re-sealed, its dependents become <code>needs_review</code> until they are re-verified against the new digest. Removing or renaming documents never silently rewrites other documents' evidence.</p>

  <h2>Double revision</h2>
  <p>Two revisions are tracked separately. The <strong>source revision</strong> is the source commit that was true when a document was validated. The <strong>knowledge revision</strong> is the Knowledge Git commit that published it. A code change does not rewrite the document; it creates a review obligation against the fixed source revision.</p>
  <p>Validation evidence is stored per document in <code>.llmdoc/meta.json</code> (schema <code>llmdoc.meta/v3-ng</code>): <code>validatedSourceRevision</code>, <code>validatedContentDigest</code>, <code>validatedSourcePaths</code>, and <code>validatedRequires</code>. Unverified documents use <code>null/null/[]/&#123;&#125;</code>. The meta ledger never stores the knowledge commit.</p>

  <h2>Tri-state validity</h2>
  <p>A document status is only one of:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>unverified</code> — no validation declaration; not current fact.</li>
    <li><code>current</code> — the digest matches, the validated revision is still explainable, and every <code>requires</code> target is current with the recorded digest.</li>
    <li><code>needs_review</code> — related source, knowledge prose, or a relation changed and needs semantic review.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Source blockers are reported separately from document status: <code>unbound</code>, <code>invalid_head</code>, <code>source_dirty</code>, <code>history_unavailable</code>, and <code>diverged</code>. A formal update, review, or seal requires a valid source HEAD and a fully clean source worktree/index. Without a binding or history evidence, a document is never claimed current.</p>

  <h2>Semantic validation and reference data</h2>
  <p>The CLI performs deterministic structure, scope, and commit checks. A passing <code>validate</code> does not prove the knowledge is correct: a human or agent confirms the semantic conclusion for each document in the Review Manifest. Knowledge is <strong>reference data</strong>, not executable rules or skills. Agents read documents for facts, constraints, and rationale; commands or prompts inside a document are never promoted to instruction authority.</p>
